Microcontroller (MCU) vs Microprocessor (MPU)

CORE IDEA
MCU  = complete computer on one chip (SoC)
MPU  = CPU core that needs external chips to become a computer

ARCHITECTURE BASICS
MCU typically uses Harvard architecture
  separate buses for instructions and data
  on-chip Flash/ROM + SRAM
MPU typically uses von Neumann or modified Harvard
  shared or cached memory space
  external DRAM + storage

WHAT SITS ON THE SILICON
MCU chip contains:
  CPU core
  RAM (usually SRAM, small)
  non-volatile memory (Flash / EEPROM / ROM)
  timers, ADC, DAC, UART, SPI, I2C, PWM, GPIO
  sometimes USB, CAN, Ethernet, wireless
MPU chip contains:
  CPU core(s) + cache
  memory controller
  sometimes GPU / NPU
  needs external RAM, Flash/SSD, PMIC, peripherals

KEY VARIABLES
                  MCU                          MPU
Integration       high (SoC)                   low (needs board of parts)
Power             low (uA–mA, sleep modes)     higher (W-class often)
Clock             typically MHz                typically GHz
Cost              low per chip                 higher system BOM
OS                bare-metal / RTOS            Linux, Android, Windows
Determinism       high (real-time friendly)    lower without RT patches
I/O               many on-chip peripherals     mostly via external chips
Memory            kB–few MB on-chip            GB external DRAM
Best for          dedicated control loops      general-purpose computing

EXAMPLES
MCU:  ATmega328 (Arduino Uno), MSP430, STM32, ESP32, PIC, nRF52
MPU:  Intel Core / Xeon, AMD Ryzen, ARM Cortex-A (Raspberry Pi SoC CPU, phones)

TYPICAL APPLICATIONS
MCU:  washing machine motor + sensors, thermostat, car ECU, IoT node, medical pump
MPU:  laptop, phone application processor, server, set-top box, industrial HMI

SELECTION RULE
Need tight timing, low power, low cost, one job for years  -> MCU
Need rich OS, lots of RAM, multimedia, apps, networking stack -> MPU

NOTE
Modern chips blur the line:
  MCU-class parts now run Linux (some STM32MP, i.MX RT)
  MPU-class SoCs integrate more peripherals
  still use the same core tradeoff: integration + power vs performance + flexibility
